    "question": "In the relational model, which statement correctly defines a domain?",
    "options": {
      "A": "The set of all possible tuples that can appear in a relation.",
      "B": "The allowed set of atomic values that an attribute may take.",
      "C": "A collection of attributes that uniquely identify a tuple.",
      "D": "A constraint that enforces referential integrity between two tables."
    },
    "correct_answer": "B",
    "explanation": "A domain is the permitted set of atomic values for an attribute, as stated in the notes. It does not describe tuples, keys, or referential constraints.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following lists the three main steps used to compute a minimal cover for a set of functional dependencies?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Decompose each FD into single‑attribute right sides; eliminate extraneous attributes; remove redundant dependencies.",
      "B": "Compute attribute closure; identify candidate keys; merge equivalent dependencies.",
      "C": "Apply Armstrong’s axioms; derive all implied FDs; select a minimal subset.",
      "D": "Normalize to 3NF; then to BCNF; finally remove transitive dependencies."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "The notes specify the minimal‑cover procedure: (1) split right sides, (2) delete extraneous left‑side attributes, (3) discard redundant dependencies.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Given the relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with functional dependencies A → B C, C → D, D → E, which attribute set is a candidate key?",
    "options": {
      "A": "{A}",
      "B": "{C}",
      "C": "{A, C}",
      "D": "{B, D}"
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "Starting with A, the closure A⁺ = {A, B, C, D, E} (using the given FDs). Since the closure contains all attributes and no proper subset of {A} exists, A is a candidate key.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Why can the HAVING clause use COUNT(*) while the WHERE clause cannot?",
    "options": {
      "A": "HAVING is evaluated after grouping, so aggregate functions like COUNT are defined; WHERE is evaluated before grouping.",
      "B": "HAVING automatically ignores NULL values, whereas WHERE treats NULL as a regular value.",
      "C": "COUNT(*) is a scalar function that only works in HAVING, not in any other clause.",
      "D": "WHERE can only reference columns from a single table, while HAVING can reference multiple tables."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "HAVING filters groups after the GROUP BY operation, when aggregates such as COUNT(*) are available. WHERE filters rows before any grouping, so aggregates are undefined there.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Consider the query: SELECT L.id, R.value FROM LeftTable L LEFT OUTER JOIN RightTable R ON L.id = R.id. If a row in LeftTable has no matching id in RightTable, what will be the value of R.value in the result?",
    "options": {
      "A": "The value from the previous row of RightTable.",
      "B": "Zero (0) for numeric columns and empty string for text columns.",
      "C": "NULL, indicating the absence of a matching tuple.",
      "D": "The query will raise an error because of the missing match."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "A LEFT OUTER JOIN retains all rows from the left side; when there is no matching right‑side tuple, the right‑side attributes are filled with NULL, as described in the notes.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  }
]
